cholars do read him to be relying on autonomy notions in On Liberty despite his explicit and emphatic categorization of his argument as utilitarian.32 Alan Ryan, for example, concludes that
Mill argues for freedom and individuality as parts of happiness rather than merely means to happiness. As a result of this, freedom in the sense of individual moral autonomy appears as a good which is valued for its own sake, because it is part of the happiness of the self-consciously progressive man.33
